// contrib/http/arena.mere — release a request's allocations when it ends.
//
// Mere's default region is a bump arena with no free, so on a long-lived
// server every request's working memory is kept forever: the parsed
// body, the strings a handler concatenated on the way to a response, and
// every string the host wrote in (the request line, the headers, each
// database column value). A server that answers a million requests keeps
// a million requests' worth of garbage.
//
// A request is the one place the lifetime is obvious, so this marks the
// arena on the way in and the host rewinds it once the response has been
// copied out. Measured on a handler that builds a 200-piece string:
//
//   without    162,840 bytes per request, never reclaimed
//   with          ~0   bytes per request
//
// Usage — outermost, so it covers the other middleware too:
//
//   let handle = router routes not_found in
//   http_serve 8080 (with_arena (with_access_log (with_cors "*" handle)))
//
// The one rule: nothing a handler allocates may outlive the response.
// That holds for ordinary request/response work and does NOT hold for a
// handler that stashes a value in something longer-lived — an in-memory
// session store, a cache, a subscriber list. Such a handler must build
// those entries from values allocated before the mark, or go without
// this wrapper. It is opt-in for exactly that reason.

extern fn http_arena_mark: unit -> unit;

let with_arena = fn (inner: str -> str) -> fn (req: str) ->
  let _ = http_arena_mark () in
  inner req;
